The agent builds UI; the user needs to see it and say what is wrong with it. Crusor
Preview opens the UI the session built in its own desktop window, lets the user click
the exact element they mean, collects the fixes, and hands the batch back to the Agent
as one scoped turn.

Flow (the nine steps), and where each lives:

  1  "a preview is available" in the Agent UI   browser/crusorAIViewPane.ts (availability chip)
  2  a SEPARATE desktop window opens            browser/crusorAIPreviewService.ts (auxiliary window)
  3  the window renders the agent's UI          media/crusor-preview/index.html frames the proxy
  4  one button arms inspect-and-edit           media/crusor-preview/preview.js
  5  hovering wireframes the element            media/crusor-preview/overlay/*.js (box, brackets, guides)
  6  clicking opens a panel anchored on it      (same file: .cp-panel placed on the element)
  7  fixes accumulate as numbered pins          (same file: .cp-pin)
  8  Review, then Send to Crusor                media/crusor-preview/preview.js (tray + sheet)
  9  the turn lands on the Agent                browser/crusorAIPreviewService.ts (prompt frame)

Engine side (Python, stdlib only):

  src/preview/inject.py    the document rewrite that makes any target inspectable: a
                           <base href> under the proxy prefix, the overlay script, and CSP
                           widening when the page ships its own policy. Idempotent.
  src/preview/server.py    the loopback proxy: per-target capability token, framing headers
                           dropped, workspace confinement, off-origin redirects refused.
  src/preview/prompt.py    the scoped turn text (label + selector + grep-able fingerprint +
                           geometry + styles + scope rules) and the v2 `prompt` frame that
                           carries it, with a preview_batch receipt for the renderer.
  src/preview/endpoint.py  the handshake file ($CRUSORAI_HOME/preview/<session>.json) so the
                           IDE is never told a port or a token by hand.

Two decisions worth recording, because both were forced by the host rather than chosen:

  * The overlay is INJECTED into the page, not imported by it. The previewed page is the
    user's own, on its own origin, so a cross-origin parent cannot touch its DOM; the proxy
    rewrites the document and the two sides talk over postMessage instead.
  * The window's chrome is our own page loaded from the workbench origin, because the
    workbench document's CSP frames only 'self' and vscode-webview: — a frame pointed at
    http://localhost:5173 is refused before the dev server is asked. The target is registered
    with the proxy by the window (its CSP allows loopback; the workbench's does not).

Design system: the overlay and the window paint with the derived --crusorai-* tokens
(crusorTheme.ts), never a palette of their own; the overlay additionally measures its own
wireframe against the element's backdrop and enforces WCAG 1.4.11 (3:1) with the same
lightness-only math, because a mid-grey outline on a white page is a feature that silently
does nothing. Icons are real SVG sprites; no emoji anywhere in the chrome.

No protocol change: a preview turn is an ordinary Bridge Protocol v2 `prompt` frame.

Verification: 57 tests (src/tests/test_preview_*.py) — injection idempotency and the
comment-false-positive that shipped in a draft, the proxy over real sockets (injection,
framing headers, token gate, workspace confinement, redirect policy, unreachable upstream),
the prompt builder (verbatim instruction, fingerprints, truncation, frame shape), the
endpoint handshake (atomic writes under concurrent readers, session ids are never paths),
and the cross-language contract (message literals pinned in both directions, node-driven
checks of the pure halves, and the SVG-only/no-hardcoded-palette rules).

preview-lab/ runs the whole flow in a browser with the real feature code on the path and
only the model turn mocked, for machines that cannot launch the desktop app.

